{"authored" [{"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)={csc}^{2}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 0 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-\\cot(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=2\\pi $ and velocity $v=4$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 4 & =2(1)+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\pi .$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+K \\\\ 2\\pi  & =-2(0)+\\pi +K \\\\ K & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer