question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-81439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2{e}^{2t}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x={e}^{2}$ and velocity $v={e}^{2}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-81439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & ={e}^{2(1)}+C \\\\ {e}^{2} & ={e}^{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)={e}^{2t}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+K \\\\ {e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+K \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-81439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1526818878b338f09a0b10dd95e7144a796de3d2346c1a541b0d58843e869c6"} {"question" "q-81439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-81439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2{e}^{2t}+t{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}"] ["math" "x(t)=2{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+t{e}^{2}+{e}^{2}"] ["math" "x(t)={e}^{2t}+t{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-81439" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=2\\pi $ and velocity $v=4$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 4 & =2(1)+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\pi .$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+K \\\\ 2\\pi  & =-2(0)+\\pi +K \\\\ K & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1526818878b338f09a0b10dd95e7144a796de3d2346c1a541b0d58843e869c6"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi "] ["math" "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4t"] ["math" "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi "] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t+3\\pi "] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-84244" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+2t+\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=-7,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=30$ when $t=4,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-7t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(4)=30.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =-7(4)+C \\\\ 30 & =-28+C \\\\ C & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-7t+58.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1526818878b338f09a0b10dd95e7144a796de3d2346c1a541b0d58843e869c6"} {"question" "q-351554" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351554" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7t+58"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)={csc}^{2}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 0 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1526818878b338f09a0b10dd95e7144a796de3d2346c1a541b0d58843e869c6"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351724" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-\\cot(t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{-2t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=0$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{-2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{-2}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{4}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1ff2ef8018fc60e875bd04c24d2fead5129d7"} {"question" "q-351732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1526818878b338f09a0b10dd95e7144a796de3d2346c1a541b0d58843e869c6"} {"question" "q-351732" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067260/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ec99f55e004c6acb5458987c0b1f